---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/projects/ProjectHeader.astro';
import FilingsDiagram from '../../components/projects/diagrams/FilingsDiagram.astro';
import { getProject } from '../../data/projects';

const project = getProject('filings-extraction-finetune');
const pendingSystems = [
	'Base Qwen3-4B, 2-shot',
	'SFT (LoRA)',
	'SFT + DPO',
	'SFT + DPO, guided JSON',
	'Teacher (gemini-3.8-flash)',
];
---

<Layout
	title={`${project.title} | Emad Gohari`}
	description={project.description}
	image={project.ogImage}
	imageAlt={project.title}
	type="article"
>
	<article class="w-full py-16 fade-in-up">
		<ProjectHeader project={project} />

		<div class="mb-10 rounded-xl border border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-500/10 px-5 py-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
			<strong class="text-text-primary">GPU results pending.</strong>
			The data pipeline, training, evaluation and serving code is written, tested and smoke-tested end to end on
			CPU with a 135M model. The full-size fine-tuning and vLLM benchmarks still need GPU runs, so this page makes
			no claim about fine-tuned quality yet. The only real-data numbers so far come from a teacher-labelling pilot
			and a rule-based baseline.
		</div>

		<div class="prose">
			<h2>The problem</h2>
			<p>
				US proxy statements (SEC form DEF 14A) contain a "Security Ownership of Certain Beneficial Owners and
				Management" table: who owns how many shares of which class, and what percentage. It is exactly the data an
				ownership graph needs, but the tables are messy HTML with footnotes, continuation rows, multi-line names
				and inconsistent columns.
			</p>
			<p>
				The goal is a <strong>small open model</strong> that turns that section into strict JSON (one record per
				holder and share class, with shares, percent, officer or director flag and role) where every record carries
				an <code>evidence_quote</code> that must be a <strong>verbatim substring of the filing</strong>, so that
				every extracted number can be checked mechanically. The output feeds the ownership graph of the
				<a href="/projects/fincrime-investigator-agent/">investigation agent</a>.
			</p>

			<h2>What I built</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					An EDGAR pipeline that samples DEF 14A filings, converts HTML tables to one pipe-delimited row per line
					and <strong>locates the ownership section by scoring headings</strong> by the numeric rows that follow
					them, skipping tables of contents and "incorporated by reference" stubs.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Teacher distillation behind a verifier</strong>: a teacher LLM labels sections with a JSON schema,
					and a deterministic verifier keeps a label only if every quote is a substring of the input, names and
					numbers appear in their own quote, values are in range and there are no duplicates.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Training</strong> of Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 with LoRA / QLoRA SFT on a completion-only loss,
					on-policy DPO pairs ranked by the verifier and F1, and an optional GRPO run with the verifier as reward.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Serving and evaluation</strong> with vLLM (merged weights, LoRA adapter, 4-bit; with and without
					JSON-schema guided decoding), a benchmark client for throughput, TTFT and p95 latency against concurrency,
					and an evaluation harness against the base model, the teacher and the rule-based parser.
				</li>
				<li>Colab notebooks for each GPU stage, and a CLI review tool for building a human-verified gold set.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Architecture</h2>
			<FilingsDiagram />

			<h2>Results so far</h2>

			<h3>Teacher pilot on real filings</h3>
			<!-- Source: filings-extraction-finetune/results/pilot/pilot_summary.json and results/pilot/label_stats_v1.json, label_stats_v2.json -->
			<div class="table-wrap">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr><th>Measurement</th><th class="num">Value</th></tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>Random DEF 14A filings probed (2016-2025)</td><td class="num">50</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Ownership section located</td><td class="num">45 / 50</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Teacher</td><td class="num">gemini-3.8-flash, JSON schema</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Verified labels, first run</td><td class="num">31 / 40</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Verified labels after fixes (thinking budget, zero-width spaces)</td><td class="num">35 / 40</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Re-verified after fixing a verifier false positive</td><td class="num">36 / 40</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Mean tokens per document, in / out (incl. thinking)</td><td class="num">3,076 / 1,571</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Teacher cost</td><td class="num">$0.33 for 40 docs ($8.20 per 1k)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
					<caption>Missed sections were investment-fund joint proxies and wrapper documents. Cost at the teacher's introductory price, October 2026.</caption>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				The verifier caught real teacher errors that a schema check would accept: a number rewritten without
				thousands separators, a quote from a continuation row that omits the holder, and <code>0</code> shares where
				the filing shows <code>-</code>.
			</p>

			<h3>Extraction quality</h3>
			<!-- Source: filings-extraction-finetune/results/pilot/heuristic_vs_teacher/metrics.json (heuristic row);
			     other rows are pending GPU runs (README.md "Extraction quality") -->
			<div class="table-wrap">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>System</th>
							<th>Reference</th>
							<th class="num">n</th>
							<th class="num">JSON valid</th>
							<th class="num">Verifier pass</th>
							<th class="num">Holder F1</th>
							<th class="num">Field F1</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>Rule-based table parser (no ML)</td><td>teacher labels (pilot)</td><td class="num">35</td><td class="num">1.00</td><td class="num">0.74</td><td class="num">0.710</td><td class="num">0.637</td></tr>
						{pendingSystems.map((s) => (
							<tr><td>{s}</td><td>gold set</td><td class="num">150</td><td class="pending" colspan="4">pending GPU run</td></tr>
						))}
					</tbody>
					<caption>The rule-based row is agreement with teacher labels on the 35 verified pilot documents, not accuracy against human gold labels (none exist yet).</caption>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				Serving numbers (peak output tokens/s, TTFT and latency p95 at concurrency 8, JSON validity for merged
				bf16, guided decoding, LoRA adapter and 4-bit variants) are also pending a GPU. The benchmark client is
				tested against a mock OpenAI-compatible server, but mock numbers say nothing about vLLM, so none are shown
				here.
			</p>

			<h2>Key design decisions and trade-offs</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Tables become one pipe-delimited row per line.</strong> A holder's name and numbers end up
					adjacent, so the natural evidence quote is the row, and grounding becomes a substring check instead of
					fuzzy matching.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Verifier before training data.</strong> The same deterministic check is the label filter, the DPO
					ranker, the RL reward and an eval metric. It cannot check semantics (for example the total column versus
					the owned-only column), which is why a human-verified gold set is part of the plan.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Model choice</strong>: Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 is Apache-2.0, ungated, a plain causal LM with
					mature LoRA, vLLM and structured-output support. Other 3-4B models were compared on licence and
					architecture.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>On-policy DPO pairs</strong> from 8 SFT samples per prompt. When no sample is good the teacher
					label is the chosen response; tied prompts are skipped. The reference model is the SFT model with the DPO
					adapter disabled, so no second copy sits in GPU memory.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The RL reward has a coverage term</strong>, because an empty <code>&#123;"holders": []&#125;</code>
					passes every verifier check. A test pins this.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Splits by company</strong>, so no issuer appears in both train and test, and over-length examples
					are dropped rather than truncated.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Limitations</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>GPU results are pending; nothing here claims a fine-tuned quality number yet.</li>
				<li>
					The verifier proves grounding, not correctness. Teacher mistakes that are grounded but wrong pass into
					training. Until the gold set is reviewed, metrics are "agreement with the teacher".
				</li>
				<li>
					Keeping only verified labels drops the hardest tables (continuation rows, multi-line names), so training
					data is somewhat easier than the test set.
				</li>
				<li>Sections are capped at 16k characters, and investment-fund proxies are out of scope.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Data and attribution</h2>
			<p>
				Filings: <a href="https://www.sec.gov/edgar" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC EDGAR</a> (public
				domain), accessed under SEC's fair-access policy with a declared User-Agent, a request-rate limit and a
				local cache. Models: Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 and SmolLM2-135M-Instruct (Apache-2.0). Teacher outputs are
				subject to the model provider's terms; the repository ships only a handful of fixture labels.
			</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>
